# Shell scripts and git hooks MUST keep LF endings: a CRLF shebang (#!/bin/sh\r)
# is an invalid interpreter on Linux/CI and breaks the SEC-02d pre-commit hook +
# the SEC-02e self-test scripts. Force LF regardless of core.autocrlf.
*.sh text eol=lf
kanz/.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Go and proto sources MUST keep LF for the same class of reason: gofmt emits LF,
# so a CRLF checkout (core.autocrlf=true on Windows) makes `gofmt -l` flag every
# file and breaks `make fmt` / `make lint` — the difference is line endings, not
# formatting. The repo already stores LF; this pins the working tree to match so
# a Windows clone lints clean. See CLAUDE.md, “Windows environment”.
*.go text eol=lf
*.proto text eol=lf

# YAML must keep LF for the same reason, and it bit us. Generated manifests are
# compared BYTE-FOR-BYTE against their generator's output:
# test/arch/tenant_compute_test.go re-renders infra/deploy/tenants/<t>/oms-<t>.yaml
# from the live base and fails on any difference. internal/tenantgen emits LF, so a
# CRLF checkout (core.autocrlf=true on Windows) makes the guard report DRIFT on a
# file that is byte-identical in git — 4892 bytes in the object, 5073 on disk,
# exactly one extra byte per line. The failure names the wrong cause ("re-render
# and commit"), and re-rendering "fixes" it by writing LF back, which hides the
# real problem until the next checkout.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
